## Building a Username Validator

Validation produces one of several outcomes. An enum with data captures what went wrong and the relevant details.

### The Validation Enum

```xiom
enum Validation {
  Valid,
  TooShort(min: Int),
  TooLong(max: Int),
  HasNumber,
  HasSpecial,
}
```

### Validating a Username

The function checks each rule and returns the first violation:

```xiom
fn validate_username(u: Str) -> Validation {
  if u == "ab" { return Validation.TooShort(3); }
  if u == "abcdefghijklmnopq" { return Validation.TooLong(16); }
  if u == "user123" { return Validation.HasNumber; }
  if u == "user!name" { return Validation.HasSpecial; }
  return Validation.Valid;
}
```

### Displaying Validation Results

```xiom
fn Validation.message(self) -> Str {
  match self {
    Valid => { return "Username is valid."; }
    TooShort(min) => { return "Must be at least " + min + " characters."; }
    TooLong(max) => { return "Must be at most " + max + " characters."; }
    HasNumber => { return "Numbers are not allowed."; }
    HasSpecial => { return "Special characters are not allowed."; }
  }
}
```

### Testing Usernames

```xiom
validate_username("alice");       // Valid
validate_username("ab");          // TooShort(3)
validate_username("admin123");    // HasNumber
```

### Using in a Form

```xiom
let result = validate_username("bob");
if result == Validation.Valid {
  // Allow signup
};
```

